% Live Answer Card — shared project preamble
% Included at the top of every module prompt in prompts/.
% This file contains ONLY what the code generator needs: product behavior,
% frozen interfaces, and code rules. No scheduling, no team or demo strategy.

PRODUCT

A sales call is in progress. When the system hears a technical question it can
answer from the company's product docs (the `corpus/` directory), it shows the
answer on a second screen as two or three large keywords the rep can glance at
and say out loud. Keywords, not sentences.

Two constraints decide everything:

1. SPEED. The end-to-end budget from utterance to rendered card is about
   3 seconds. An answer that arrives late is worthless.
2. SILENCE. The system shows nothing unless it is confident. Small talk,
   pricing, scheduling, and anything it cannot answer well produce the designed
   empty state. A wrong keyword is much worse than a blank one. Silence is a
   deliberate output, not a failure.

FROZEN INTERFACES

These signatures and dict keys are frozen. Do not rename them, change
arguments, or add/remove dict keys, even if a cleaner design is visible.

    audio.get_utterance() -> dict
        {"text": str, "speaker": "prospect"|"rep"|"unknown", "ts": float}

    trigger.should_fire(utterance: dict) -> dict
        {"fire": bool, "question": str, "reason": str}
        reason in {"technical","smalltalk","repeat","not_a_question"}

    retrieval.answer(question: str) -> dict
        {"keywords": list[str], "detail": str, "source": str, "confidence": float}

    screen.render(card: dict) -> None
        draws the card; below 0.6 confidence draws the designed empty state

    model_client.fast_complete(prompt: str, max_tokens: int = 200) -> str
        provider-agnostic; returns "" on any error or timeout

The entry point is app.py, a Streamlit script that owns the polling loop.
There is no main.py — Streamlit re-runs its script rather than being driven
from outside.

Only model_client may import a model provider SDK. Every other module calls
fast_complete(). Modules communicate by direct function call inside one
process — no database, no HTTP endpoints of our own.

CODE RULES — apply to every generated module

1. A lane function NEVER raises. Wrap the body in try/except, print the error,
   and return the empty version of the module's contract.
2. Every module defines USE_MOCK at the top. When True it returns hardcoded
   data and touches no network. All tests must pass with USE_MOCK = True and
   no network available.
3. Every module prints one line per call with its input and key output, in the
   module's documented log format.
4. Plain Python. No web framework beyond Streamlit, no async, no class
   hierarchies, no speculative abstractions.
5. Every network call sets an explicit timeout at or under its latency budget.
6. Credentials come from environment variables loaded from .env (see
   .env.example). Never hardcode a key, never print one, never place one in
   example code.

LATENCY BUDGETS — every model/network call carries an explicit timeout at or
below its stage budget; a timeout produces the empty value, never a wait

    mic input -> committed transcript      < 1000 ms
    fire decision, cheap-path rejection    <   50 ms
    fire decision, model-classified path   <  400 ms
    vector search                          <  100 ms
    keyword generation                     < 1400 ms
    screen render                          <  100 ms
    end to end                             < 3000 ms

PRODUCT DECISIONS — fixed, do not revisit

- Confidence threshold for showing a card: 0.6
- Keywords per card: at most 3 (2–3 typical), each glanceable, not a sentence
- End-to-end latency budget: ~3 seconds
- Pricing questions never fire the card; that conversation belongs to the rep.
- Optimisation target for the fire decision: precision over recall. A missed
  question is a small loss; a card fired during small talk erodes the rep's
  trust in the screen. Bias every threshold accordingly.
